module ir_image;
import ntasm_pe;

// ===========================================================================
// THE BYTE CONTAINER FOR AN IR IMAGE. This is the artefact ntir.nova and
// machine_ir.nova did not have, and why they did not need one is worth stating
// before the layout, because it is the whole justification for the file.
//
// Both IRs are a `buf<u64>`: host words, handed to the harness by pointer,
// never written down. ntir.nova:44-63 and machine_ir.nova:63-76 each specify a
// header, and each reader refuses a wrong tag or a wrong version
// (ntir.nova:840-841, machine_ir.nova:2774-2775 and :2960-2961). That is real,
// and for the NTIR it is exercised: tests_ntasm_ntir.c:576-577 damages the magic
// and the version and demands 808.
//
// It is also, on its own, a header with nothing outside the process to be wrong
// about. A u64 buffer has no byte order, no record alignment, no length and no
// digest; it is a C ABI and it is exactly as portable as the compiler that made
// it. The measure of the state this file was written from is one line:
//
//     grep -rn deserial --include=*.nova --include=*.c --include=*.h .  -> 0 hits
//
// No reader of an IR image had ever existed, in any language, anywhere in this
// repository. The rejection paths above could only ever be reached by a caller
// that already had the buffer in its hand, which is to say by the process that
// had just written it.
//
// So this is NOT a second schema competing with the one inside ntir.nova. The
// layout of the RECORDS stays exactly where it is and stays the sole property of
// the module that owns it. This file owns the ENVELOPE: how a header and a run
// of u64 records become bytes, and how bytes become a header and a run of u64
// records again.
//
// IT DUPLICATES NO RECORD-LAYOUT CONSTANT, and that is the design decision worth
// arguing about, because the obvious alternative is for this module to also
// carry each image's header_words and node_words. It does not, for two reasons.
// First, the stride and the count are ALREADY in the image's own header — NTIR
// word 5 is NODE_WORDS and word 3 is node_count (ntir.nova:47-63) — and
// ntir.verify already refuses a wrong one at 814, which tests_ntasm_ntir.c:578
// exercises under the name "stride wrong". Second, a second copy of a constant
// is a second thing to keep in step, and nxo.nova:18-35 exists to explain what
// happens to this project when the version lives in two places: object.c and
// nxo.nova each held the same fact twice and object.c had no way to notice they
// disagreed. The container therefore knows NOTHING about what an NTIR is. It
// hands back u64 words and stops. Deciding whether those words are an NTIR is
// the job of the module that owns the NTIR, and it is ntir.verify's job alone.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// WHAT IS COPIED FROM nxo.nova, AND WHY. The NXD object format already answered
// "how does a versioned container refuse a wrong input", and it answered it well,
// so the mechanism is reused rather than reinvented:
//
//   1. THE TAG IS DERIVED FROM THE VERSION (nxo.nova:36-59). `version_digit`
//      computes the four digits from FORMAT_VERSION and the tag is a rendering
//      of the version rather than a second copy of it, so the eight tag bytes
//      exist in exactly one function and a writer cannot emit a tag that
//      disagrees with the version it wrote. Copied in shape, including the rule
//      that an out-of-range index is 0 so a caller cannot read past the tag and
//      believe it read a version.
//
//   2. A LOOP OVER tag_byte, NEVER A LIST OF LITERALS (nxo.nova:62-68). Read and
//      emit ask the same question of the same function, so they cannot hold two
//      different opinions about what this format is called. This is the direct
//      consequence of copying 1, and it is the reason the mechanism is worth
//      copying rather than the tag bytes being worth copying.
//
//   3. RESERVED WORDS, WRITTEN ZERO AND REFUSED NON-ZERO (nxo.nova:82-85
//      `zeros`, :181 read, :252 `clear`). Copied, including the reason nxo.nova
//      gives at :88-91: the writer zeroes them rather than trusting the
//      allocator, so the emitted bytes are a property of this module and not of
//      whatever memory the runtime handed back.
//
//   4. THE SIZE CROSS-CHECK (nxo.nova:186). The reader compares the length the
//      header claims against the length it was actually given and refuses a
//      file that is short OR long. A truncated file must never read as a
//      shorter one.
//
//   5. REFUSE BEFORE ALLOCATE (nxo.nova:206). Every fixed field is checked
//      before one record is materialised, so a corrupt file costs a rejection
//      and not an allocation whose size was read out of the corrupt file.
//
//   6. A REFUSAL IS A CODE AND AN EMPTY BUFFER, NEVER A PARTIAL OBJECT
//      (nxo.nova:68-74 `bad_read`/`bad_write`). Copied, so a caller that ignores
//      the code gets something it can harmlessly free rather than a
//      half-populated image it has no way to know is incomplete.
//
// WHAT IS NOT COPIED, and the omission is deliberate: the section table.
// nxo.nova:187-196 carries three section records because an object file has
// sections. An IR image is one flat run of records, so a section table would be
// a structure with exactly one possible contents; adding it would be design for
// its own sake, and it is the reason nxo's header is 240 bytes and this one is
// 56.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE LAYOUT. Little-endian throughout, fixed width, no padding, no alignment
// requirement on the body.
//
//    0   8  tag         NTI0 or MIR1, then the four decimal digits of
//                      FORMAT_VERSION. Byte 3 is the DOMAIN as an ASCII digit:
//                      '0' is NTIR, '1' is Machine IR. See note 2 above for why
//                      the domain appears both here and at offset 12.
//    8   4  version     u32. FORMAT_VERSION, again as a number.
//   12   4  domain      u32. DOMAIN_NTIR or DOMAIN_MACHINE_IR.
//   16   4  word_bytes  u32. Always 8, and REFUSED otherwise. A reader willing
//                      to accept a word size it does not have is a reader that
//                      will misread the body, so this is a rejection and not a
//                      tolerance.
//   20   4  reserved0   u32, must be zero.
//   24   8  word_count  u64. Total u64 records in the body, image header
//                      included. Redundant against the image's own words 3 and
//                      5 on purpose: it is a second and independent statement of
//                      the same length, so a body and a header that disagree are
//                      refused before either is believed.
//   32   8  size        u64. Total bytes. Must equal the length handed to read.
//   40   8  digest      u64. FNV-1a over every byte of the file except bytes
//                      40..48, which are this field.
//   48   8  reserved1   u64, must be zero.
//   56   -  body        word_count u64, little-endian.
//
// WHY THE DIGEST, and it is the one field here that is not a structural check.
// Every other field is a claim a reader can settle by comparison against a
// constant or against the length it was given. The digest is a claim about
// CONTENT, and it is the field that turns a buffer into an artefact: before it,
// two IR images were comparable only by walking both of them inside one process
// that had to hold both alive at once. It is FNV-1a because machine_ir.nova:
// 837-846 already uses FNV-1a with these exact two constants, and a tree with two
// FNV implementations and two different digests for the same name is a tree that
// will one day disagree about which is which.
//
// WHAT THE DIGEST DOES NOT DO, stated because a digest that is only ever
// compared against a value the same code just computed proves nothing at all.
// It detects corruption and accidental divergence. It is NOT a signature: a file
// that has been maliciously rewritten, digest and all, is accepted. A signature
// is a different artefact with a different threat model and none is claimed here.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE CODES, and why they are a fresh band. 8xx is spoken for up to 844 and 9xx
// from 930, so 850..860 are unused across the whole tree. They are distinct from
// every IR refusal code on purpose: a caller that gets 851 must be able to say
// "this file is a revision I do not know" WITHOUT the possibility that the same
// number came out of a node verifier, and the two are different failures with
// different repairs. Collapsing them would have made the failure unattributable,
// which is the defect class this project keeps finding.
// ===========================================================================
// Only the READ direction returns an owned buffer, and only because a reader
// cannot know the caller's buffer is big enough before it has read the count
// out of the file. `write` returns a code and fills the caller's own buffers.
export struct ReadResult { code:u64,words:buf<u64>,word_count:u64 }
// THE VERSION OF THE ENVELOPE. One number, and the tag's last four bytes are
// derived from it rather than typed beside it, so a version and a tag that
// disagree are not expressible. That is nxo.nova:36-59's mechanism reused, and
// it is reused rather than reinvented as a fresh pair of constants because the
// second copy is the defect, not the number.
export fn FORMAT_VERSION()->u64 { return 1; }
export fn HEADER_BYTES()->u64 { return 56; }
export fn WORD_BYTES()->u64 { return 8; }
export fn TAG_LENGTH()->u64 { return 8; }
export fn DOMAIN_NTIR()->u64 { return 0; }
export fn DOMAIN_MACHINE_IR()->u64 { return 1; }
export fn MAX_BYTES()->u64 { return 67108864; }
export fn MAX_WORDS()->u64 { return (MAX_BYTES()-HEADER_BYTES())/WORD_BYTES(); }

export fn E_TAG()->u64 { return 850; }
export fn E_VERSION()->u64 { return 851; }
export fn E_DOMAIN()->u64 { return 852; }
export fn E_WORDBYTES()->u64 { return 853; }
export fn E_RESERVED()->u64 { return 854; }
export fn E_SIZE()->u64 { return 855; }
export fn E_COUNT()->u64 { return 856; }
export fn E_DIGEST()->u64 { return 857; }
export fn E_TRUNCATED()->u64 { return 858; }
export fn E_INPUT()->u64 { return 859; }
export fn E_CAPACITY()->u64 { return 860; }

export fn domain_known(domain:u64)->bool {
    if domain==DOMAIN_NTIR() { return true; }
    return domain==DOMAIN_MACHINE_IR();
}

// ---- the tag, derived -----------------------------------------------------
// The digit of FORMAT_VERSION at `index`, most significant first, as ASCII.
// Out of range is 0, so a caller cannot walk off the end of the tag and come
// back with a version it invented. This is nxo.nova:42-48's function, for the
// reason nxo.nova:18-35 gives.
fn version_byte(index:u64)->u64 {
    if index>3 { return 0; }
    let mut divisor:u64=1000;
    let mut step:u64=0;
    while step<index { divisor=divisor/10; step=step+1; }
    return 48+(FORMAT_VERSION()/divisor)%10;
}
// Byte `index` of the tag. Bytes 0..2 are the family, byte 3 is the domain as a
// digit, bytes 4..7 are the version. One function answers for all eight, so the
// tag cannot exist in two places -- which is the entire point of deriving it.
export fn tag_byte(domain:u64,index:u64)->u64 {
    if index>=TAG_LENGTH() { return 0; }
    if index==0 { if domain==DOMAIN_NTIR() { return 78; } return 77; }
    if index==1 { if domain==DOMAIN_NTIR() { return 84; } return 73; }
    if index==2 { if domain==DOMAIN_NTIR() { return 73; } return 82; }
    if index==3 { return 48+domain; }
    return version_byte(index-4);
}

// THE FAMILY: the three letters and the domain digit. A failure here is E_TAG
// -- the reader was handed something that is not an IR image at all, or is the
// other IR. It is checked separately from the version on purpose, because "not
// our format" and "our format, a revision we do not know" are different
// failures and a caller that cannot tell them apart cannot report either.
fn family_ok(domain:u64,input:buf<u8>)->bool {
    if len(input)<4 { return false; }
    let mut i:u64=0;
    while i<4 { if input[i]!=tag_byte(domain,i) { return false; } i=i+1; }
    return true;
}
// THE VERSION DIGITS: a failure here is E_VERSION. The family was already
// right, so the file IS an IR image of a revision this reader does not know.
fn version_ok(input:buf<u8>)->bool {
    let mut i:u64=4;
    while i<TAG_LENGTH() { if input[i]!=version_byte(i-4) { return false; } i=i+1; }
    return true;
}

// ---- the digest -----------------------------------------------------------
// FNV-1a over an explicit byte range, skipping the digest field itself. The
// skip is the only subtlety: hashing the field being written would be a fixed
// point nobody solves, so the field is written zero, hashed around, and then
// filled in.
//
// The range is an ARGUMENT rather than len(), and that is not a detail. `write`
// emits into a caller-supplied buffer whose capacity is not the file's length,
// and hashing the capacity would make the digest a property of the BUFFER
// rather than of the image -- the same image would then carry a different
// digest depending on how much room the caller's allocator happened to have.
fn digest_range(input:buf<u8>,size:u64)->u64 {
    if size<HEADER_BYTES() || size>len(input) { return 0; }
    let mut h:u64=14695981039346656037;
    let mut i:u64=0;
    while i<size {
        if i>=40 && i<48 { i=i+1; continue; }
        h=h^(input[i]);
        h=h*1099511628211;
        i=i+1;
    }
    return h;
}
// The digest of a complete file, for a reader that was handed the whole thing.
export fn digest(input:buf<u8>)->u64 { return digest_range(input,len(input)); }

// ---- refusals -------------------------------------------------------------
// A refusal is a CODE, and on every refusal the returned buffer is empty. The
// caller must still free it. That is nxo.nova:68-74's rule and the reason for
// it is worth restating: a half-populated image is the one return value a
// caller has no way to recognise as incomplete, so this module never produces
// one.
fn bad_read(code:u64)->ReadResult {
    let empty:buf<u64>=alloc(runtime(),0);
    return ReadResult{code:code,words:empty,word_count:0};
}

// ---- write ----------------------------------------------------------------
// The image is a `buf<u64>` of `count` records, which is exactly what
// ntir.build and machine_ir.lower fill. The container does not look at one of
// them: it is the wrong module's job to know what an NTIR record means, and a
// container that checked a record would have to duplicate the IR's own layout
// constants to do it.
//
// `out` and `length` are the CALLER's buffers. That is the convention
// ntir.serialize and machine_ir.serialize already use, and the one that
// matches the modules this container actually serves. nxo.emit allocates and
// returns its own buffer instead, which is the right shape for an object file
// and the wrong one here: the caller here is a harness that already owns a
// scratch buffer and would have to free a second one per fixture.
export fn write(domain:u64,words:buf<u64>,count:u64,out:buf<u8>,length:buf<u64>)->u64 {
    if !domain_known(domain) { return E_DOMAIN(); }
    if count==0 || count>MAX_WORDS() { return E_COUNT(); }
    if len(words)<count { return E_INPUT(); }
    if len(length)<1 { return E_INPUT(); }
    let size:u64=HEADER_BYTES()+count*WORD_BYTES();
    if size>MAX_BYTES() { return E_SIZE(); }
    if len(out)<size { return E_CAPACITY(); }
    let mut i:u64=0;
    while i<TAG_LENGTH() { out[i]=u8(tag_byte(domain,i)); i=i+1; }
    ntasm_pe.put32(out,8,FORMAT_VERSION());
    ntasm_pe.put32(out,12,domain);
    ntasm_pe.put32(out,16,WORD_BYTES());
    ntasm_pe.put32(out,20,0);
    ntasm_pe.put64(out,24,count);
    ntasm_pe.put64(out,32,size);
    // Bytes 40..48 are the digest and are filled in LAST, once every other byte
    // of the file exists. Bytes 48..56 are reserved and are written zero rather
    // than left to whatever the caller had in that buffer: `out` belongs to the
    // caller, so it may hold anything, and an emitted file that inherited a
    // stale reserved word would be a file this module refused to read back.
    ntasm_pe.put64(out,40,0);
    ntasm_pe.put64(out,48,0);
    let mut j:u64=0;
    while j<count {
        ntasm_pe.put64(out,HEADER_BYTES()+j*WORD_BYTES(),words[j]);
        j=j+1;
    }
    ntasm_pe.put64(out,40,digest_range(out,size));
    length[0]=size;
    return 0;
}

// ---- read -----------------------------------------------------------------
// EVERY FIXED FIELD IS CHECKED BEFORE `alloc` IS CALLED. `count` is read out of
// the file and is the size of the allocation a naive reader would make on the
// strength of it, so nothing is allocated until `count` has been compared
// against two independent facts: the length of the file this reader was given,
// and the ceiling above. This is nxo.nova:206's rule, and it is the rule that
// makes reading a hostile file safe rather than merely careful.
export fn read(domain:u64,input:buf<u8>,capacity:u64)->ReadResult {
    if !domain_known(domain) { return bad_read(E_DOMAIN()); }
    let size:u64=len(input);
    if size<HEADER_BYTES() { return bad_read(E_TRUNCATED()); }
    if size>MAX_BYTES() { return bad_read(E_SIZE()); }
    if !family_ok(domain,input) { return bad_read(E_TAG()); }
    if !version_ok(input) { return bad_read(E_VERSION()); }
    // The version is checked TWICE, in the tag and as the number at offset 8,
    // and the two are compared against each other as well. That is redundant on
    // purpose: the tag is derived from FORMAT_VERSION at run time, so a file
    // that satisfies one of these and not the other could not have been written
    // by this module. It can therefore only be an EDITED file, and the point of
    // the cross-check is to notice exactly that.
    let version:u64=ntasm_pe.read32(input,8);
    if version!=FORMAT_VERSION() { return bad_read(E_VERSION()); }
    if ntasm_pe.read32(input,12)!=domain { return bad_read(E_DOMAIN()); }
    if ntasm_pe.read32(input,16)!=WORD_BYTES() { return bad_read(E_WORDBYTES()); }
    if ntasm_pe.read32(input,20)!=0 { return bad_read(E_RESERVED()); }
    if ntasm_pe.read64(input,48)!=0 { return bad_read(E_RESERVED()); }
    let count:u64=ntasm_pe.read64(input,24);
    if count==0 || count>MAX_WORDS() { return bad_read(E_COUNT()); }
    if ntasm_pe.read64(input,32)!=(size) { return bad_read(E_SIZE()); }
    // The body must be exactly what the header says it is, to the byte. This
    // is what makes a truncated file a refusal: without it, a file cut in half
    // would arrive as a shorter but internally consistent image.
    if HEADER_BYTES()+count*WORD_BYTES()!=(size) { return bad_read(E_COUNT()); }
    if count>capacity { return bad_read(E_CAPACITY()); }
    if ntasm_pe.read64(input,40)!=(digest(input)) { return bad_read(E_DIGEST()); }
    // Past this line every number used has been compared against a constant,
    // against the length of the input, or against the file's own other copy of
    // the same fact. Allocating is the first moment any of them is believed.
    let out:buf<u64>=alloc(runtime(),count);
    let mut i:u64=0;
    while i<count {
        out[i]=ntasm_pe.read64(input,HEADER_BYTES()+i*WORD_BYTES());
        i=i+1;
    }
    return ReadResult{code:0,words:out,word_count:count};
}

// ---- the shapes, for a reader that wants to name what it is holding -------
// These are the ENVELOPE's fields and nothing else. The image's own header
// word 0 (the IR magic) and words 1 and 2 (its major and minor) are NOT read
// here and are not named here: they belong to ntir.nova and machine_ir.nova,
// which already publish and already check them, and a second reader of them
// would be a second answer to a question two modules already answer.
export fn size_of(input:buf<u8>)->u64 { return ntasm_pe.read64(input,32); }
export fn word_count_of(input:buf<u8>)->u64 { return ntasm_pe.read64(input,24); }
export fn digest_of(input:buf<u8>)->u64 { return ntasm_pe.read64(input,40); }
